    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having a massive headache with our jenkins pipeline. we've got these incredibly flaky unit tests in the staging environment. the code hasn't changed, but the build stage fails intermittently with weird, non-deterministic errors in the logs. sometimes it's a timeout, sometimes it's a weird connection reset. any idea how i can start isolating if this is an environmental issue or something else?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "First, you should review your Jenkins environment and compare its stability against a known-good staging environment. Check for any variations in configurations, network connectivity, and storage. It might be helpful to examine the CI logs for recent changes around the same time as the intermittent build failures. How stable is your staging environment compared to a production one? Are there any wild changes in the size and number of builds? What does your Jenkins Job Summary show compared to your Production Builds table? Have you run any recent jobs? How have the health of your Jenkins environment, your Linux machines, Maven, the database, and other Jenkins plugins fared in the last two weeks? For example, how many failed builds have you seen? Check JenkinsPipelineStageExecutor, try escalating a build step outside Jenkins, and check the status of that step by seeing if there is an ACT_UI step directly before a failed step. Was Jenkins failing non-Jenkins steps? Were you running the pre-test smoke test? Can you determine what changed on the last successful build by diffing them from the failing ones? Consider running an alternative CI build in parallel (using Travis CI, GitHub Actions, Bitbucket Pipelines, GitLab CI, etc.) for the remaining steps—but pause at each step",
      "source": "target",
